ure that's  coming soon is dynamic Geographics where  we figured for this particular thing it  makes sense not to use the whole box  rendering stack that would be used by a  test of Mathematica but you have our own  custom JavaScript implementation for  from apps okay  so there you have a map with some  polygons on top of it and you can  interact with this pain around zoom in  and so on and this is this is working in  cloud notebooks now I think the function  is still experimental anyway but if you  want to start using maps in notebooks  that's probably an interesting thing  finally I want to talk a little bit  about typesetting  in relation to the editor and the sad  news is that we don't support it yet  completely so you know in desktop  Mathematica you have a very nice editor  we can enter subscript superscripts  fractions grids and so on  we're still working on the foundation to  make that happen in in web browsers as  well it's just not not easy at all but  in the meanwhile I thought I'd show a  few things how you could sort of work  around that and one thing we've had for  a while I think is a way to convert from  input form to standard form so input  form is a thing where I would just type  X /y  whereas in standard form this turns into  a fraction an actual typeset fraction  and this is in the sell gear menu under  convert to standard form and derogate a  fraction so that's nice if you have a  single you know a whole cell that  contains some math that you could  represent this way but I can't even take  this now so I can select the cell  bracket and then press command C to copy  it to the clipboard and it could paste  this anywhere else for instance in a new  input cell and then I can can also edit  around that so I can have something like  this I can paste it another time I just  can't cursor inside of this construct it  stays as an atomic blob but I can delete  it as a whole if I want to and the nice  thing is that this has of course that  the right semantics so if I evaluate  this it realizes this is not just an  image or anything but it it is actually  the fraction x over Y we don't yet but  that's also coming yeah that would help  with this too  evaluate the place yes so that would do  the other way and we're just about to  hook up shortcuts for this as well you  don't have them yet because it's again a  bit tricky in the browser it won't let  us use the ones we want but we'll find  other ones  another thing that's coming probably not  too soon but not too far away either I  hope is a way to input tech code which  probably many people are familiar with  and that's that's also a nice way to  write formula and it's also not it's  also easier for us because we only have  to worry about a linear input string as  opposed to this 2d typeset typesetting  editor so the idea is that you press  command for or command dollar and then  you get this little input field  similarly to control equals for  linguistic input and here I can now type  backslash fraction XY and when I resolve  it it also turns into the typeset  construct and again this has the right  semantics of course so that's that's  kind of becoming as well I think this  will probably land in the cloud first  because it's more important for us  because there is no other good way to  input typesetting constructs but I think  it is definitely planned for desktop as  well I think it will be a nice features  in it should yeah  yep huh and yep that's a few things  we've been working on it's a very active  area of development so I couldn't show  everything here's a few related talks  about the compiler if you're interested  in that or just other front-ends or  notebooks in general and a shameless  blog if you're interested in more  practical applications of cloud  notebooks and how to build something  like a blog with them I'll do another  talk on Friday about that thank you very  much  [Applause]

